        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Fixing a damp basement starts with a two-day plastic sheet test and a $15 hygrometer. Moisture on the room side means humid air: close the windows in summer, run a dehumidifier at 45 to 50 percent, vent the dryer outside and insulate cold pipes. Moisture on the wall side means wet soil: fix gutters and grading, then seal the walls.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Clear out cardboard and old carpet, clean small mold patches safely, and give the basement a full wet season before finishing it. Most damp basements are dry for good with a few hundred dollars and a little patience.
          </p>
